# systemd units for network bring-up - MULE
#
# The execution half of roadmap item 1.2. mule/bringup.py holds the ORDER;
# os/config/networkd.conf.template holds the CONFIGURATION; this describes the
# units that run them and, more importantly, what they do not have to do.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Unit names carrying an interface are TBD, because interface
# naming is TBR-LINUX-01 and open. This is an input to configuration
# generation, not a file to be dropped in /etc/systemd/system.
#
# FML-ADR-059 owns the split: systemd-networkd owns link configuration and mesh
# attachment, wpa_supplicant and hostapd own association, nothing else may
# reconfigure a link.

# --- Most of the ordering is not yours to enforce ---------------------------
#
# THIS IS THE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS, and earlier files in this repository
# overstate the problem, including FML-ADR-059's own consequences.
#
# systemd.network(5), on ConfigureWithoutCarrier=:
#
#   "Allows systemd-networkd to configure a specific link even if it has no
#    carrier. Defaults to false."
#
# So networkd already waits. A .network file with BatmanAdvanced= does not
# apply while the interface has no carrier, and a mesh point interface has no
# carrier until wpa_supplicant has joined the mesh. The hazard the ordering
# exists for -- batman-adv attached to an interface that is not yet up, failing
# in a way that looks like a radio fault -- is prevented by networkd's default
# behaviour rather than by a unit dependency somebody has to remember.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THESE UNITS: there is no "attach after association" unit
# to write. What there is instead is a setting NOT to change.
#
#   DO NOT set ConfigureWithoutCarrier=true on a mesh member interface.
#
# It is the line that turns the hazard back on, it looks like a fix when a link
# is slow to come up, and it would be applied by someone debugging exactly the
# symptom it causes.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-30, and it did not need to wait for Stage 2. Two
# mac80211_hwsim radios: a mesh point brought up but not joined reports
# carrier 0, and reports carrier 1 after joining. So the gating is real. See
# docs/evidence/TBR-LINUX-01/2026-08-30-80211s-mesh-in-software.md.
#
# What remains unverified is narrower: whether a REAL driver behaves the same
# way. mac80211_hwsim is mac80211's own simulator and a vendor driver is free
# to assert carrier earlier. Worth checking on the first board that arrives,
# and cheap to check: bring the interface up, read carrier, join, read again.

# --- Association: wpa_supplicant --------------------------------------------
# Upstream ships wpa_supplicant@.service, a template unit taking an interface.
# Use it rather than writing one: FML-ADR-023 records that the upstream MANET
# reference project encodes real bring-up knowledge, and a hand-rolled unit
# discards whatever the distribution's has learned.
#
# enable=wpa_supplicant@TBD.service       # TBR-LINUX-01 for the interface name
# config=/etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant-TBD.conf
#
# The configuration is os/config/wpa_supplicant.conf.template. What matters
# here is that the unit is enabled for the mesh interface and that networkd is
# not also trying to associate, which it cannot: it has no such capability, and
# that is why this component exists at all.

# --- The access point: hostapd ----------------------------------------------
# enable=hostapd.service                  # or hostapd@TBD.service per distro
#
# FML-ADR-045 keeps the access point and the mesh as separate radio FUNCTIONS
# whether or not they share a radio, and TBR-RF-03 decides whether they do. If
# they share one, the unit set is not two independent services and this file is
# wrong; that is the thing to re-read when that trade closes.

# --- Services wait for the mesh, not for the network ------------------------
# The last ordering rule in mule/bringup.py is mesh_interface_up before
# services_started. Expressed as a dependency on the interface being
# configured, not on network-online.target, which means something weaker:
#
# [Unit]
# After=systemd-networkd-wait-online@TBD.service
# Requires=systemd-networkd-wait-online@TBD.service
#
# where TBD is the mesh interface. systemd-networkd-wait-online@.service is an
# instantiated unit that waits for ONE link, which is what is wanted: a node
# whose access point is up but whose mesh is not should not be starting
# services that assume peers.
#
# WHICH services is not decided here and is not this file's to decide.
# FML-ADR-035 puts service policy in services/service-controller/, which is
# blocked on TBR-HA-01. mule/bringup.py has one step called services_started
# and says the same thing.

# --- The LoRa plane is not in this file -------------------------------------
# It has its own supervisor requirement and its own reasons; see
# os/config/meshtasticd.conf.template. It is not ordered against the mesh
# because it does not depend on it: FML-ADR-026 makes it the plane that still
# works when the IP plane does not, and a unit ordering that made the lifeline
# wait for the mesh would defeat the decision.
